# hello.a68 - ALGOL 68 Hello World.
  The hash symbol starts a comment and the next hash symbol ends it. The same
  symbol opens and closes the comment, so the Algol 68 Genie manual says
  comments cannot nest. Two other pairs do the same job: CO ... CO and
  COMMENT ... COMMENT. Comments can sit almost anywhere, but not in the
  middle of a symbol. #

# A program is a particular program: an enclosed clause that runs inside the
  standard environment. The Genie manual shows that a one-line program is
  complete, so there is no main function and no BEGIN ... END around it. #

# print is a procedure of the standard prelude, the library that every
  Algol 68 program can use without importing anything. It takes one
  argument: a row of values to write. #
# That is why the call has two pairs of parentheses: the inner pair builds
  the row, here the string "Hello, World!" followed by new line, and the
  outer pair is the procedure call. A string is written between double
  quotes. new line is a value of the standard prelude that starts a new
  line, so the output ends with a newline and the shell prompt does not
  follow the text. #
print (("Hello, World!", new line))

# Compared with C or Pascal: there is no main function, no braces, no
  semicolon after the last statement, and no include line. #
